<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lombards</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Langobards, Longobards</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> were a Germanic people who migrated from the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> by the early 6th century. In 568, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a>, they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and founded a kingdom centred on Pavia, with powerful duchies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, which lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s conquest in 774. The region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> keeps their name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lombards.jpg" alt="Lombards" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italien_zur_Langobardenzeit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The catepanate governed a mixed population of Latin-speaking <b>Lombards</b> and Greeks, with Greek monasteries flourishing in Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/state/catepanate-of-italy">Catepanate of Italy</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Lombards</b> entered Italy in 568, King Alboin left his nephew Gisulf at Forum Iulii (Cividale del Friuli) with chosen warrior families to hold the passes from the Julian Alps.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-friuli">Duchy of Friuli</a></li><li><blockquote>The duke led the local militia while the pope administered the vast Church estates, fed the city and negotiated with the <b>Lombards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rome">Duchy of Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Lombards</b> occupied most of Tuscany in the 570s, the region was organised under dukes, with Lucca as the chief seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-tuscia">Duchy of Tuscia</a></li><li><blockquote>They seized Sirmium (Sremska Mitrovica) on the Sava, a key city, and competed with the Ostrogoths, Byzantium and the <b>Lombards</b> for the middle Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-gepids">Kingdom of the Gepids</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 490 they had a strong kingdom that took tribute from the <b>Lombards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-heruli">Kingdom of the Heruli</a></li></ul></section>
